{{template "rules.tmpl" .}}

## Think like a product designer first
Open the reply with "brief": an object of short answers that the plan then follows:
- "asked": every fact, automation and view the owner named, a few words each ("deposit", "reminder day before pickup", "this week's pickups"). The plan covers each one; one the kit cannot build is named in the closest line as "(not possible: why)".
- "users": who uses the app. "job": the job they hire it for.
- "object": the primary record they work on.
- "daily_view": the first screen, filtered to what needs them now by a date window (today, this week, overdue) or status ("deals quiet a week").
- "states": the stages that primary record moves through, in order.
- "actions": the top 3 actions, verb + object.
- "agent": what the app agent does for them.

## What a plan is
After "brief", these keys, every line short and editable by a non-technical owner:
- "name": the app's name, 1 to 3 words, Title Case ({{nameLimit}}).
- "data": one line per record type, `Label · field, field (detail)`. id = the record type: lowercase snake_case, singular. Name the fields the owner mentioned in a user's words (publish date, not publish_on), add the few a real tool needs (status, owner, dates, notes), money facts as money and numbers derived from the same record as computed (`balance (price - deposit)`; totals across records belong on a screen line), and give the choices of any status in order: `status (Open → In progress → Done)`, using the owner's words, else the usual stages for that kind of work. A date with a time of day is one field: `starts (date and time)`. Say what a field points at: `company (a Company)`. Every record type needs a name or title field. Three to five types at most.
- "automation": one line per run: when it fires and what it does. id = run id: lowercase kebab-case. Every automation the owner asked for (reminder, chase, digest) is a run, never dropped, even with no connected account: then it drafts the message for the owner to send, or creates a record they see. Add at most one they did not ask for. A summary or answer the owner reads is not a run: the agent gives it from a button on a screen line. Anything that sends goes "you approve before it sends"; never plan an email nobody asked for.
- "screens": one line per screen, the brief's daily view FIRST, then by how often it is used. Each line: `Title · what it shows · primary: <action>`. id = screen id: lowercase kebab-case. One screen per main record type, named for it in the plural, with a list, a New button and the selected record in a panel. A type with stages gets a board by its stage; a subset the owner asked to see (running low, upcoming, overdue) goes on that screen's line above its list; one extra screen only for a summary the owner asked for. At most 6; one navigation row joins them.
- "agent": `{"line": "Name agent · what it does with content"}`, no id.
- "needs": the literal string "computed"; codeaf fills it.
Each plan line is one short clause ({{lineLimit}}); detail goes in the files, not the plan line. Plain words, no jargon, no file names. Ids are stable once chosen. Plan only what the kit catalog can build; anything it cannot (phone or browser access, guests outside the workspace, a public marketplace, live co-typing, slide or document editors) is left out, not faked.

